  <section class="brx-sec" id="open-vent">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Why do St Helens WA10 systems get so much sludge?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">Many WA10 Victorian terraces have open-vented heating. This type of system rusts faster than a modern sealed one, so it fills with sludge sooner.</p>
      <p>An open-vented system has a small tank in the loft. It tops the heating up as water is lost. Each top-up brings in fresh water full of oxygen. Oxygen reacts with the steel in your radiators. The result is black iron oxide sludge.</p>
      <p>A sealed system, like most combis, keeps the same water going round. Once its oxygen is used up, rust slows right down. So a 15-year-old open-vented system often holds far more sludge than a sealed one of the same age.</p>
      <h3>Why the downstairs radiators go first</h3>
      <p>Sludge is heavy. It settles at the lowest points of the system. In a St Helens terrace, that is often the front room and kitchen. Upstairs can be fine while downstairs is barely warm. That pattern almost always means sludge, not a boiler fault.</p>
      <h3>How we flush open-vented systems</h3>
      <ul class="sg-ticks">
        <li>We isolate the loft tank, so dirty water cannot come back in.</li>
        <li>We flush any gravity circuits on their own.</li>
        <li>We flush every radiator one at a time, with the flow reversed.</li>
        <li>We set the inhibitor dose for the size of the system and tank.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="signs">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Does my St Helens heating need a power flush?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">The clearest sign is a radiator that is warm at the top and cold at the bottom. If you spot two of the signs below, it is worth a check.</p>
      <ul class="sg-ticks">
        <li><strong>Ground floor radiators cold at the bottom.</strong> The most common pattern we see in WA10 terraces.</li>
        <li><strong>Black water when you bleed a radiator.</strong> That is iron oxide sludge, and it is going through your boiler.</li>
        <li><strong>A radiator that will not heat at all.</strong> The flow path is fully blocked. It is common in old systems that have never been flushed.</li>
        <li><strong>The house takes far longer to warm up.</strong> Flow gets weaker as pipes clog.</li>
        <li><strong>A banging or kettling boiler.</strong> Water is boiling in a blocked heat exchanger.</li>
        <li><strong>You bleed radiators every few weeks.</strong> Rust inside makes gas, so the air keeps coming back.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="brx-note"><p><strong>When a flush will not help:</strong> it will not fix a broken pump or a failed boiler part. If your boiler keeps losing pressure, that is a leak, and it needs finding first. We check this before we suggest a flush.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="how">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>How long does a St Helens power flush take?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">Most St Helens homes take one day. Open-vented systems take a bit longer, because of the extra steps.</p>
      <div class="brx-table-wrap">
        <table class="brx-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Home</th><th>Typical time</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Sealed combi system, 3-bed home in WA11 or WA12</td><td>5 to 6 hours</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Open-vented WA10 terrace, 7 to 10 radiators</td><td>6 to 8 hours</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Very dirty open-vented system, never flushed</td><td>A full day</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <h3>What happens on the day</h3>
      <ol class="sg-steps">
        <li><strong>Check and test (20 minutes).</strong> We find out if the system is sealed or open-vented. We check for weak spots and test the water.</li>
        <li><strong>Add the cleaner.</strong> Fernox F3 for a standard clean, or Sentinel X400 for heavy sludge. On very dirty systems, we let it work longer first.</li>
        <li><strong>Isolate the tank and connect (30 minutes).</strong> On open-vented systems we deal with the loft tank. Our Kamco CF90 Quantum2 pump then goes on.</li>
        <li><strong>Flush each radiator (most of the day).</strong> Every radiator is flushed on its own, with the flow reversed. On the worst systems the water starts jet black.</li>
        <li><strong>Flush until clear.</strong> We run until the water comes back clean, then test it again.</li>
        <li><strong>Inhibitor, balance and report (30 minutes).</strong> We add inhibitor to BS 7593, balance the radiators and give you a written report.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="brx-cta">
        <p><strong>Know how many radiators you have?</strong> Call and we will give you a fixed St Helens price on the phone.</p>
        <a href={TEL} class="brx-btn">Call {PHONE}</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="right">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Is a power flush right for my St Helens home?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">If your system is over 10 years old with cold radiators or black water, usually yes. If it is newer with mild signs, a £195 chemical clean may do.</p>
      <div class="sg-cols">
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>A flush is a good fit if</h3>
          <ul class="brx-list">
            <li>You have an open-vented system with cold spots</li>
            <li>Your downstairs radiators are cold at the bottom</li>
            <li>The system is over 10 years old and never cleaned</li>
            <li>You are fitting a new boiler to your old radiators</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Talk to us first if</h3>
          <ul class="brx-list">
            <li>Your boiler keeps losing pressure (that is a leak)</li>
            <li>You still have a back boiler</li>
            <li>Your pipes and radiators are badly rusted</li>
            <li>Only one radiator is cold</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>In very old pipework, sludge can harden into flakes. A longer chemical soak first often works better. On badly rusted systems, new radiators may be the better buy. See our <a href="/radiators-st-helens">St Helens radiator prices</a>, from £245 supplied and fitted.</p>
      <p>We will tell you which job you need, not sell you the bigger one.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="new-boiler">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Do I need a power flush when I replace my back boiler?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">Yes, in almost every case, if you keep the old radiators. Boiler makers need the system cleaned and treated to BS 7593, or they can refuse a warranty claim.</p>
      <p>Back boilers are still common in older St Helens terraces. We do not service them. We take them out and fit a modern combi in their place. Twenty or more years of sludge in the old radiators can damage a new heat exchanger in months. So we flush the system on the same job.</p>
      <div class="brx-note"><p><strong>Getting a new boiler from us?</strong> We check the system on every <a href="/boiler-installation-st-helens">St Helens boiler installation</a>. If it needs a flush, it is £350 on the same job.</p></div>
      <h3>Keeping it clean after</h3>
      <p>Fit a magnetic filter to catch new sludge. It is £150 with a flush. Then have the inhibitor checked at your yearly <a href="/boiler-service-st-helens">boiler service</a>. A clean system can go many years without another flush.</p>
      <h3>Who does the work?</h3>
      <p>Our power flushes are done by our Gas Safe registered engineers, part of our team led by James. You can check any engineer on the <a href="https://www.gassaferegister.co.uk/">Gas Safe Register</a>. Every job has a 12-month workmanship guarantee and £2 million public liability insurance.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</ServiceGuide>
